WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:02.482
(playful pop music)

00:00:02.482 --> 00:00:05.992
- Chimp Haven is a retirement facility.

00:00:05.992 --> 00:00:07.795
This is their forever home.

00:00:07.795 --> 00:00:10.506
This is where they come to explore

00:00:10.506 --> 00:00:12.910
and live like chimpanzees.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.104
(chimpanzees chattering)

00:00:17.104 --> 00:00:18.187
In June 2013,

00:00:19.187 --> 00:00:22.038
the director of the National
Institutes of Health

00:00:22.038 --> 00:00:25.508
made an announcement that
virtually all of the chimpanzees

00:00:25.508 --> 00:00:28.142
would be retired from research.

00:00:28.142 --> 00:00:32.144
We have close to 200 chimpanzees here now.

00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:36.311
Around half of our colony
are veterans of research

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:41.893
that was focused on a cure for HIV/AIDS,

00:00:41.893 --> 00:00:44.494
as well as Hepatitis C.

00:00:44.494 --> 00:00:46.344
And so, as a result of that,

00:00:46.344 --> 00:00:50.511
those chimpanzees had to be
infected with those viruses.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.417
- Good morning, Gina!

00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:58.628
Genetically, I'd say
we're about 98% identical

00:00:58.628 --> 00:00:59.984
to a chimpanzee.

00:00:59.984 --> 00:01:02.674
And, as you can imagine, that's
why they were ideal subjects

00:01:02.674 --> 00:01:04.452
in test studies because
they wanna learn more

00:01:04.452 --> 00:01:06.343
about how they can benefit humans.

00:01:06.343 --> 00:01:07.528
So, I would definitely say

00:01:07.528 --> 00:01:09.018
that the human and chimp relationship

00:01:09.018 --> 00:01:11.050
is one that is ever-evolving.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.002
Give me your hand.

00:01:12.002 --> 00:01:13.587
Give me your hand.

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:14.420
Good girl!

00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:17.880
They're a lot like us
and our family dynamics.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.548
You see siblings, they're interacting,

00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:20.841
they're playing with one another.

00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:22.162
Every relationship is different;

00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:23.606
it's just like us with humans.

00:01:23.606 --> 00:01:24.706
Certain individuals you'll have

00:01:24.706 --> 00:01:28.873
really close intimate relationships
with; some not so much.

00:01:30.396 --> 00:01:33.055
- We also have seen
some behavioral changes

00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:37.222
where a chimpanzee might
appear to be overly timid

00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:41.400
and, you know, see a change in that over time.

00:01:43.162 --> 00:01:47.916
We might see that a chimpanzee
is a lot more interested

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:52.418
in exploring the
five-and-a-half-acre habitat,

00:01:52.418 --> 00:01:54.192
and this might've been
the first opportunity

00:01:54.192 --> 00:01:56.275
that they had to do that.

00:01:58.795 --> 00:02:00.671
- I think it's such a
great benefit to know

00:02:00.671 --> 00:02:03.556
that this is where they'll live
out the rest of their lives

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.357
and to know that despite
whatever their past may be,

00:02:06.357 --> 00:02:08.820
we are at least giving
them the opportunity

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.413
to live out a time of retirement

00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:11.589
where they're with other chimps,

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:12.698
where they're able to exhibit

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:14.509
these species' typical behaviors,

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:15.984
and they're just able to be chimps

00:02:15.984 --> 00:02:17.592
and know that, you know what?

00:02:17.592 --> 00:02:18.675
Life is good.

